<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # A Dream Trip Around the World – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Exploring Our Global Community</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> A Dream Trip Around the World</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Comprehend lectures on general academic subjects. Interactive: Engage in debates on change, including local or global changes.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can comprehend main ideas in academic lectures or presentations about global travel and tourism (e.g., "The speaker explained that Machu Picchu is one of the most famous ancient sites in the world, but Chichen Itza is larger and has more well-preserved structures.")
                Can participate in debates on global cultural issues, recognizing and responding to different opinions (e.g., "Some people believe traveling sustainably is expensive, while others argue that it benefits local economies in the long run.")
                Can ask questions about global changes discussed in debates (e.g., "What ideas were the speakers sharing about how different cultures were managing tourism impacts?" During the debate, community leaders were explaining how they were balancing tourism with preserving their cultural heritage and natural environments.")</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images featuring diverse global travel destinations (e.g., pyramids, breathtaking waterfalls, sandy beaches, majestic mountains, bustling city streets). Students, in pairs, listen to each other describe which image represents their "dream destination" and briefly explain why, using initial target vocabulary like <i>adventure, experience, explore, visit</i>. The teacher asks students to think about what they would do if they *could* travel anywhere.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary words: <i>adVENture, des-ti-NA-tion, PYR-a-mids, breathtaking, magnificent, diverse</i>, emphasizing correct stress patterns. The teacher also models the second conditional in sentences like "If I <u>could</u> travel around the world, I <u>would</u> visit Egypt," focusing on vowel reduction in "could" (/kəd/) and "would" (/wəd/). The teacher says: "I will describe my dream trip. Listen carefully. If I could visit any <b>destination</b>, I would choose Egypt to see the ancient <b>pyramids</b>. It would be a truly <b>breathtaking adventure</b>."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'breathtaking adventure,' am I talking about a boring trip or an exciting one?" (Expected answer: An exciting one.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'If I could travel..., I would visit...,' am I talking about a real trip happening now or a hypothetical dream trip?" (Expected answer: A hypothetical dream trip.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Exploring Our Global Community"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Audio recording of a short (approx. 2-3 minutes) academic lecture or podcast segment titled "Global Travel: Beyond Tourism - Understanding Cultures." The segment discusses the benefits of global travel, cultural diversity, and famous destinations, integrating grammar (superlatives, past continuous) and vocabulary (<i>culture, diversity, understanding, perspectives, global, enriching, learn, encounter, share</i>). The register is informative and engaging, suitable for a B1.3 academic context.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Comprehend lectures on general academic subjects, specifically on global travel and cultural understanding.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First Listening (Gist):</b> Students listen to the lecture for the main idea. Teacher asks: "What is the main topic of this lecture? Is it about traveling for business or about the personal benefits and cultural aspects of global travel?" (Expected answer: Personal benefits and cultural aspects of global travel.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second Listening (Detailed):</b> Students listen again and complete a True/False exercise based on specific statements from the lecture, checking their understanding of details. For example: "True or False: The speaker mentioned that experiencing diverse cultures is the most important benefit of global travel." "True or False: While traveling in Thailand, the speaker was learning about traditional cooking."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Third Listening (Vocabulary Focus):</b> Students listen for and jot down 3-5 key vocabulary words from the lecture related to culture and understanding. (e.g., <i>diversity, perspectives, enriching, traditions, understanding</i>).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening for specific grammar structures (Second conditional, Superlatives, Past continuous) and target vocabulary (<i>travel, culture, experience, diversity, adventure, destination, breathtaking, global, enriching, learn, narrate, discover</i>) within spoken contexts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Listening Gap-fill:</b> Students receive a transcript of a short excerpt (30-45 seconds) from the lecture with 5-7 target vocabulary words (e.g., <i>diversity, enriching, understanding, perspectives, breathtaking</i>) and instances of second conditional, superlatives, or past continuous (e.g., <i>if I could, I would, the most beautiful, was learning</i>) omitted. They listen to the audio excerpt and fill in the missing words/phrases. Teacher plays the audio twice.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Listening for Sentence Structure:</b> The teacher plays 4-5 isolated sentences or short dialogue snippets (10-15 seconds each) related to travel experiences. Students listen and identify if each sentence uses the <b>second conditional</b> (hypothetical), a <b>superlative</b> (comparison), or the <b>past continuous</b> (narrating past action). For instance, students might hear: "If I could visit the most remote island, I would explore its unique culture." (Second conditional & Superlative). "While we were hiking, we were encountering breathtaking natural beauty." (Past continuous).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Listening and Response:</b> In pairs, students take turns listening to a partner complete specific sentences orally using the target grammar and vocabulary. E.g., Partner A says: "If I could [explore/visit] any country, I would [travel to]... because..." Partner B listens, provides feedback on grammar/vocabulary use, and then asks a follow-up question related to cultural differences or benefits of travel: "What is one <b>tradition</b> you found interesting while you were traveling?" or "How was your <b>experience</b> abroad in regards to changing your <b>perspective</b>?" This encourages active listening and pragmatic interaction.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-17 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Exploring Our Global Community"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Global Travel Talk Show" - a simulated panel discussion where students act as travel experts or recent travelers sharing their "dream trip" ideas and discussing the benefits of exploring different cultures. The teacher acts as the moderator, introducing topics and asking questions. Students form small groups (3-4) and take turns as "guests."</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group discusses a hypothetical "dream trip" to a specific region (e.g., South America, Asia, Africa). They listen to the moderator's questions about cultural understanding, sustainable tourism, and personal growth through travel, using target vocabulary (<i>diversity, culture, understanding, benefits, perspectives, traditions, educational, enriching</i>) and grammar (second conditional for hypothetical travel, superlatives for comparing places, past continuous if they "imagine" past travel experiences).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The moderator will ask questions such as: "If you could visit the most unique destination in South America, where would it be, and why would you want to experience its <b>culture</b>?" or "Many people talk about the <b>benefits</b> of an <b>Exchange Program</b>. What do you think are the <b>most enriching perspectives</b> one could gain?" or "While people were traveling <b>globally</b> in the past, what were they often <b>learning</b> about local <b>traditions</b>?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students must actively listen to the moderator and their group members, building on ideas and respectfully discussing cultural differences or travel experiences, as per pragmatic functions. They should demonstrate showing interest in classmates' stories by asking follow-up questions or acknowledging points made.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher uses an observation checklist to assess students' listening comprehension and their ability to formulate coherent answers during the "Global Travel Talk Show." Students will also submit a brief "Exit Ticket."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listening Comprehension:</b> Student accurately responds to moderator's and peers' questions, indicating clear understanding of the input.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Student incorporates at least 3-5 target vocabulary words (e.g., <i>diversity, culture, enriching, perspective, adventure</i>) naturally in responses.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Grammar:</b> Student uses second conditional, superlatives, and/or past continuous accurately when appropriate (e.g., "If I could go..., I would visit...," "The most beautiful place," "While I was learning...").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Student demonstrates intelligible pronunciation, including attempts at vowel reduction in modals and correct stress on multisyllabic words (e.g., <i>adVENture, des-ti-NA-tion</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic Competence:</b> Student respectfully discusses cultural differences and shows interest in classmates' stories by asking relevant follow-up questions.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick written reflection:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of listening to today's discussions or lecture?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"List two new vocabulary words you learned today and try to use them in a sentence about your dream trip."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Thinking about today's lesson, how has listening to different perspectives on global travel broadened your understanding of other cultures?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific strategies did you use today to help you understand the main ideas in the lecture or during the talk show?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What aspects of the listening tasks worked particularly well in engaging students and promoting comprehension? What specific challenges did students face with listening for particular grammar points or vocabulary? What adjustments or scaffolds (e.g., pre-teaching more vocabulary, providing a graphic organizer for listening, repeating audio segments) might be needed for future listening lessons, especially to improve understanding of academic lectures?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson introduced core vocabulary and concepts related to global travel through listening. In the next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension), students will engage with a written text that uses much of the same vocabulary and thematic ideas, allowing them to deepen their understanding of context and reinforce the new words and structures.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>